. . . the two of them had become very close friends and colleagues, and it was not unusual for the three of them to spend hours in the lab talking and working on their respective projects. But, of course, there was more to their relationship than just friendship. There was a certain attraction between them that neither could deny. And while their respective spouses might not approve, they were both aware that the other was not entirely averse to the idea of a little extracurricular activity, and indeed, on occasion, the three of them had indulged in some of that. However, their main focus was the work. And that was what was important to them.
However, their latest collaboration had led them into a strange and dangerous area of research, and they were now on the verge of discovering a new type of particle, a particle that could change the world forever. They knew that the government would not let them publish the results of their work, and they also knew that the military would not allow them to use the technology in the field. So, the only way to get the word out was to write a novel, a book that would describe their discoveries, a book that would tell the story of the discovery, the struggles, the dangers, and the triumphs.

