Before Coristine could elaborate further on the chromatic transgressions of the leviathan encounter, a new sound cut through the morning noise. It was a sound of impossible cheer, of saccharine menace. A tour bus, airbrushed with a grinning, winking, borderline-offensive caricature of a Native American brave, was rumbling down the street. On its side, in a font straight out of a 1970s western comic, was the legend: "Chief Running Boot's Post-Sexist Ghost Ranch: Where Our Spirits Aren't the Only Thing That's Busted!" A beardless white man in a war bonnet stuck his head out the driver's side window. "Hey there, pilgrims! Looking to ride the trail of broken hearts and plundered orifices? Chief Running Boot offers a ten percent discount to all government agents and wayward academics!"
Tingle blanched. "Running Boot," he whispered, his voice trembling. "The strip-miner. The profiteer. He doesn't care about the journey of the hero, the sacred communion, the transcendent release. He just wants to mass-produce the ghost of the orgasm, bottle it, and

{Let's try to double them. Or try quintuple?}
{Yes, the number of quantum objects, for our quantum capitalism, must be quantified. Let's say 7,777,777,777.}
{Quantum capitalism must... be... regulated?}
{No! Anarchic. Unbound!}
{Panic. Profit. Repeat.} banality of his prose could create a vacuum that our own, more sophisticated ontological narratives could fill."
Maxine held up a hand. "Wait. Let me see if I'm following this." She pointed at Chief Running Boot's bus, which was now blasting a synthesized flute version of "Apache" from its outdoor speakers. "That man," she said, "is a bad guy because he sells cheap, inauthentic butt-pounding."
"Precisely!" Tingle confirmed. "He doesn't honor the hero! He doesn't take the time to build the tension, to make the ponies really *work* for it! It's all thrust, no finesse. All... algorithmic emptiness." He shuddered. "It's a soul-crushing corporate handjob masquerading as true, passionate unicorn love."

{Wait! How? I thought we were in Manhattan!}
{Oh well. No one here's even gonna blink at an Entropy-Accountant.}
The desert heat of Tehran baked the asphalt. The dusty, ancient-looking street they suddenly found themselves on was now thronged with the adorable, paradoxical sight of trotting unicorns. Persian merchants paused in their haggling, their expressions unreadable. A man selling pistachios simply shrugged and continued calling out his prices. Here, a beam of asset-stripping energy and a shield of co-opted literary theory was perhaps just a form of particularly aggressive street theater. The fundamental truths of the marketplace, however perverted, were universal. Farnoosh, the tea-seller, poured the Accountant a small glass, its surface untouched by the man's presence, an invitation to buy property he could not occupy. What was offered could not be accepted, and what was held for sale could not be bought. The ground itself reconfigured with the logic of capitalism and a new property was born in their midst: A perfect patch of nothing, priced to move. All it required was an opening bid.
"The desert doesn't care about our problems," Tingle whispered, a note of awe in his voice.

