{The Entropy-Accountant, of course, lies.}
The Entropy-Accountant froze. The dollar sign on its featureless face flickered, its rhythm disrupted. For a nano-instant, the market stalled. The relentless calculus of acquisition encountered a variable it couldn't quantify: a gesture of pure, unadulterated, weaponized sincerity. It was a bug in the code of the cosmos. Tingle's hand, pressed against its chest, wasn't applying pressure; it was offering a data packet of pure, un-monetizable feeling.
The light from Tingle's hand didn't just glow; it *saturated* the space around them. It was the color of a unicorn's dream of a field of four-leaf clovers, shimmering with the patina of a truly heroic butt-pounding. The light seeped into the Entropy-Accountant, not as an attack, but as an infection. An infection of grace.
Inside the Accountant's internal market, chaos erupted. The ticker tape of despair halted. The stock of existential misery plummeted. The exchange rate of loneliness to consumer debt went critical. Futures in ennui were worthless.

{What do the duo do with the meatballs?}
{They look at the meatballs.}
It's the Trot. Oh, God, it's the cute, terrible Trot. His movements are economical, almost mechanical. A lift of the knee, a forward placement of the foot, a gentle bounce. There is no joy in it, no liberating release. There is only the rote perfection of the motion, like a piece of software executing its code. A tiny shimmer glimmers in the air behind him, a mirage of a gleaming white horse with its tail held high. Its body is there, but also not there, a shimmering potentiality, a dream of a pony that never quite materializes. The shimmer winks in and out of existence, a glitch in the cheap, particleboard reality of IKEA.
No one else reacts. A woman pushing a cart laden with gleaming plastic storage boxes doesn't even break her stride. A couple arguing loudly over whether to get the white or the black FLÅDIS lamp ignores him completely. The disease has become normalized. The hesitation has been engineered away. The Trot is no longer a symptom; it's a feature of the urban landscape. A ghost in the system, but now it's a pre-installed app.

